## 🏛️ SECTION 2: CLEAN ARCHITECTURE STANDARDS

### 2.1 Mandatory Layer Separation
Every feature module MUST follow this strict flow — no exceptions:

```
HTTP Request
    ↓
[Controller]         — Route handler only. No business logic. Input validated via DTO.
    ↓
[Service / Use Case] — Orchestrates domain logic. Calls repositories. Owns transactions.
    ↓
[Domain / Entity]    — Pure business rules. No framework deps. No DB deps.
    ↓
[Repository]         — Data access ONLY. Returns domain entities, not raw ORM objects.
    ↓
[Database / Cache]
```

### 2.2 Module Structure (per NestJS feature module)
```
apps/api/src/modules/<feature>/
├── dto/
│   ├── create-<feature>.dto.ts      # Input DTO (class-validator + Swagger)
│   └── update-<feature>.dto.ts
├── entities/
│   └── <feature>.entity.ts          # Domain entity (pure class, no ORM decorators)
├── repositories/
│   └── <feature>.repository.ts      # PrismaService wrapper, returns domain entities
├── <feature>.controller.ts          # @Controller — thin route handler
├── <feature>.service.ts             # Business logic orchestrator
├── <feature>.module.ts              # NestJS module wiring
└── <feature>.spec.ts                # Unit tests (Jest)
```

### 2.3 SOLID Enforcement
- **S** — Single Responsibility: Each class has ONE reason to change.
- **O** — Open/Closed: Extend via strategy/plugin patterns, never modify stable core.
- **L** — Liskov: Repositories MUST implement their interface contract fully.
- **I** — Interface Segregation: Define narrow interfaces (IUserReader, IUserWriter).
- **D** — Dependency Inversion: Services depend on repository interfaces, not Prisma directly.

